# Hard Constraints

## 1) Commit preparation gate

- "Prepare commit" means draft message and readiness checks, not immediate commit.
- Never run `git commit` or push without explicit user instruction to execute.
- Draft from actual diffs, not from guessed intent.

## 2) Planning source discipline

- Use trusted planning sources first: `docs/ai/ACTIVE_WORK.md`, `docs/ai/FEATURE_REGISTRY.md`, `docs/ai/TECH_DEBT.md`, recent `docs/ai/PROGRESS_LOG.md`, code/tests.
- Do not infer backlog from snapshot/archive/reference docs.

## 3) Refactor discipline

- Refactor work should target a clear end state.
- Avoid leaving silent dual-path temporary layers; either remove or register debt in `docs/ai/TECH_DEBT.md`.

## 4) Context recovery

When context may be stale, re-bootstrap from:
- `docs/ai/WORKFLOW_PROFILE.md`
- `docs/ai/BOOTSTRAP_DIGEST.md`
- `docs/ai/PROJECT_CONTEXT.md`
- `docs/ai/ACTIVE_WORK.md`

## 5) Comment hygiene (IDs and design section numbers stay out of code)

- **Forbidden** in source comments, log messages, test-case names, and code-adjacent inline docs:
  - Feature, Slice, Bug, ADR, or TechDebt IDs (`CORE-F01`, `CORE-F01-S02`, `BUG-CORE-001`, `ADR-20261015-01`, `TD-014`)
  - bare slice tags used as prefixes or tags (`S03:`, `(S08)`, `design S02`, `layer 2`)
  - design-document section numbers (`§3.4`, `design §2.7`, `H8`)
  - requirement / acceptance-criteria labels (`O1`, `I1`, `Q3`)
  - commit hashes, or pointers into collaboration docs ("see ACTIVE_WORK", "see design doc")
- **Doxygen style is required for contracts**: public API uses `///` with `@brief` / `@param` / `@return` / `@warning` where they add information; internal notes use `//`. Comments are written in English.
- Comment only the non-obvious *why*, invariants, hazards, and trade-offs. Do not annotate every field or method; a comment that restates the code is noise and should be deleted.
- IDs belong in `docs/ai/`, commit messages, and PR text — not in code.
- Full rule: `.cursor/rules/code-comments-no-feature-ids.mdc`.

## 6) Branch naming (topic granularity; no registry IDs)

- Feature branches use topic paths: `feat/<topic>` or `feat/<topic>/<subtopic>` (e.g. `feat/render`, `feat/render/material`, `feat/gameplay`).
- Name the **area of change**, not a Feature/Slice row. Prefer an existing matching area branch over a new ID-tied branch.
- **Forbidden:** branch names built around registry IDs (e.g. `feat/core-f01-xxx`, `feat/core-f01-s01`).
- Same ID ban applies to `fix/` / `refactor/` / `chore/` (and similar): topic words only.
- The workflow profile cannot disable this section or the comment rule in section 5.
